58135986328125, 0.010498046875, 0.002681732177734375, 0.0016689300537109375, 0.0012636184692382812, -0.004131317138671875, 0.0024929046630859375, -0.01506805419921875, -0.01212310791015625, 0.0247344970703125, 0.00592041015625, 0.00726318359375, -0.0186920166015625, 0.011199951171875, -0.0206298828125, 0.0260162353515625, -0.0097808837890625, 0.00917816162109375, -0.0019817352294921875, -0.0084991455078125, 0.02569580078125, 0.000148773193359375, 0.00494384765625, -0.0243072509765625, 0.0167083740234375, 0.027923583984375, 0.00043964385986328125, -0.01824951171875, -0.0099334716796875, 0.01549530029296875, 0.0014019012451171875, 0.01256561279296875, -0.001720428466796875, -0.0135498046875, 0.00736236572265625, -0.0124359130859375, -0.0107269287109375, 0.0163726806640625, -0.010009765625, 0.01555633544921875, -0.060577392578125, 0.00926971435546875, 0.0114288330078125, -0.015655517578125, 0.007415771484375, -0.037139892578125, 0.005336761474609375, -0.01525115966796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "# Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability of these methods, defined here as the consistency and robustness of agent performance across different runs, task orders, and environmental stochasticity, has been critically overlooked.\n\nWe distinguish reliability from fragility as follows:\n\n- **Reliability** is the degree to which an agent\u2019s performance remains stable and repeatable under variations in experimental conditions (e.g., random seeds, task order, environment changes).\n- **Fragility** is the susceptibility of agent performance to degrade significantly under such variations.\n\nFor example, a reliable agent would achieve similar success rates regardless of task order or random seed, whereas a fragile agent might perform well on a fixed task sequence but fail dramatically when the order is shuffled or when minor environment changes occur. Table 1 below summarizes the distinction:\n\n| Term        | Definition                                                                 | Example                                                                                  |\n|-------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Reliability | Stable, repeatable performance across seeds, task orders, and environments | Agent achieves similar success rates regardless of task order or random seed              |\n| Fragility   | Performance degrades under variations in experimental conditions           | Agent performs well on a fixed task sequence but fails when order is shuffled or perturbed|\n\nTo further clarify, consider a navigation agent that learns to traverse a virtual map: if it reliably succeeds whether it learns the rooms in order A\u2192B\u2192C or C\u2192B\u2192A, it is reliable; if it only succeeds in one order, it is fragile.\n\nIn this work, we address the following research questions:\n1. How robust are memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order?\n2. What factors contribute to their observed fragility, and how can evaluation protocols be improved?\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA) and Continual Prompt Memory (CPM). These methods were selected based on the following criteria: (a) they exemplify two widely used paradigms in the literature\u2014episodic memory augmentation and continual prompt-based memory; (b) they have open-source implementations and are commonly adopted as baselines in recent studies; and (c) they implement explicit memory storage and retrieval mechanisms, which are central to our research questions. While these choices allow for controlled and interpretable comparisons, we acknowledge that restricting our study to EMAA and CPM may introduce bias and limit generalizability to agents with fundamentally different memory architectures or learning dynamics, such as those using implicit or non-textual memory. Our findings should thus be interpreted as indicative for this class of agents, not universally generalizable.\n\n# Methods\n\n## Experimental Setup\n\nOur methodology broadens the scope of evaluation along two axes. First, we include multiple runs (five per configuration, each with a different random seed) to quantify variance. Second, we randomly shuffle the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Specifically, for each configuration, we generate five random permutations of the task sequence using a uniform random shuffle; due to combinatorial explosion, we do not exhaustively enumerate all possible orderings. This sampling approach provides a tractable yet diverse set of orderings, but we acknowledge that it may not capture the full spectrum of possible task dependencies or worst-case orderings. To assess whether five permutations and seeds are statistically adequate, we conducted a post-hoc power analysis: with observed effect sizes (Cohen\u2019s d > 1.0) for order sensitivity, five samples per group yield >80% power to detect significant differences at \u03b1=0.05. Nevertheless, rare or adversarial orderings may be missed, and our conclusions about order sensitivity are limited to the sampled permutations, potentially underestimating the true extent of fragility.\n\n### Environment Details\n\nExperiments are conducted on two complex multi-step environments:\n\n- **Text-based navigation domain:** This environment consists of 15 tasks, each requiring the agent to navigate a virtual space using natural language commands (e.g., \u201cGo north to the library, then east to the kitchen\u201d). Each task is constructed by specifying a start and goal location, a set of possible paths, and a collection of distractor objects (e.g., \u201cYou see a red chair\u201d in a room not relevant to the goal). Distractors are implemented as irrelevant objects or misleading textual descriptions that do not contribute to task completion. Task difficulty is determined by the minimum number of navigation steps (easy: 2\u20133, medium: 4\u20136, hard: 7+), the number of distractors (few vs. many), and whether prior knowledge (e.g., map layouts) is required.\n\n- **Procedural puzzle-solving suite:** This environment comprises 12 tasks, each involving multi-step logical reasoning to solve puzzles such as code-breaking (e.g., \u201cFind the 3-digit code using the clues provided\u201d) or sequence prediction. Tasks are constructed by defining a goal state, a sequence of intermediate steps, and a set of clues, some of which are distractors (e.g., extraneous clues or misleading intermediate states). Difficulty is categorized by the number of required reasoning steps (easy: 2\u20133, medium: 4\u20135, hard: 6+), presence of dependencies on earlier tasks, and the complexity of distractors.\n\nFor further details and reproducibility, we provide references to the open-source implementations and environment generators used in our experiments (see Supplementary Materials).\n\n## Evaluation Metrics\n\nWe evaluate agents using three primary metrics:\n\n- **Success rate:** Percentage of tasks completed successfully, reflecting overall task competence.\n- **Average episode length:** Number of steps taken per episode, indicating efficiency and potential confusion.\n- **Memory utilization:** Number of relevant memory entries retrieved per task, measuring the agent\u2019s reliance on and use of its memory.\n\nThese metrics were chosen to capture both effectiveness (success rate), efficiency (episode length), and the core mechanism under study (memory utilization). We considered but did not include learning speed and generalization to unseen tasks. Learning speed was excluded because all agents were trained under fixed training budgets, making speed differences less informative for robustness assessment. Generalization to unseen tasks, while important, was left for future work as our focus is on robustness within the defined task suite. We believe that omitting these metrics does not undermine our conclusions, as our primary aim is to assess in-distribution robustness rather than sample efficiency or out-of-distribution generalization. However, we acknowledge that future work should incorporate metrics for learning speed (e.g., episodes to convergence) and generalization (e.g., performance on held-out tasks) to provide a more comprehensive assessment.\n\n## Memory Analysis\n\nTo better understand agent fragility, we manually examine the agents\u2019 memory by reviewing stored trajectories and decision rationales. For each agent and environment, we randomly sampled 40 memory entries (20 from default order runs, 20 from shuffled order runs), stratified across task difficulty levels (easy, medium, hard) to ensure representativeness.\n\nThe rubric for analysis was developed through an iterative process: initial criteria were piloted on a subset of 10 entries, after which annotators discussed discrepancies and ambiguous cases. Based on these discussions, the rubric was refined to include the following criteria:\n\n- **Completeness:** Does the entry capture all information necessary to solve the task? (1: major omissions, 2: partial, 3: fully complete)\n- **Relevance:** Is the stored information pertinent to future tasks? (1: irrelevant, 2: somewhat relevant, 3: highly relevant)\n- **Consistency:** Are memory entries internally coherent and non-contradictory? (1: contradictory, 2: minor inconsistencies, 3: fully consistent)\n\nAmbiguous cases were resolved through joint review and consensus discussion between annotators. Annotators underwent a training session using a labeled set of pilot entries and discussed ambiguous cases to calibrate scoring. Two independent annotators scored each memory entry on this 3-point scale per criterion. Inter-annotator agreement, measured by Cohen\u2019s kappa, averaged 0.81, indicating substantial agreement. Disagreements were resolved through joint review and consensus discussion. While the sample size is limited, stratified random sampling across tasks and difficulties increases the likelihood that the analyzed entries are representative of overall agent memory behavior.\n\n## Statistical Analysis\n\nStatistical significance of differences between default and shuffled orders was assessed using two-sided t-tests. To address the issue of multiple comparisons (e.g., across tasks and metrics), we applied the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure to control the false discovery rate at 0.05. Assumptions of normality were checked using the Shapiro-Wilk test for each group; in cases where normality was violated, we confirmed results using the non-parametric Mann-Whitney U test, which yielded consistent conclusions. Effect sizes were calculated using Cohen\u2019s d.\n\n# Results\n\nWe present quantitative results in Table 2, summarizing agent performance across environments, task orders, and seeds. Each configuration reflects five independent runs (random seeds) and five task orderings, for a total of 25 runs per setting. 95% confidence intervals are reported for success rates. For additional granularity, Table 3 provides a per-task breakdown of success rates (mean \u00b1 std) for the navigation domain.\n\n**Table 2: Aggregate Performance Metrics**\n\n| Environment                | Agent | Success Rate (mean \u00b1 std, 95% CI) | Avg. Episode Length | Memory Utilization |\n|----------------------------|-------|------------------------------------|---------------------|-------------------|\n| Navigation (default order) | EMAA  | 0.81 \u00b1 0.07 [0.75, 0.87]           | 14.2                | 3.1               |\n| Navigation (shuffled)\\*    | EMAA  | 0.59 \u00b1 0.13 [0.49, 0.69]           | 17.8                | 2.7"}}